{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-100631",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x+2{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 3{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
